# Unified Fitting User Guide

The line-cut plotter and `Tools > Fit Traces` now use the same model editor,
parameter parser, optimizer, result formatting, and `maxfev` setting.

## Model expressions

The independent variable is `x`. An expression can contain:

- Python arithmetic operators such as `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and `**`.
- NumPy functions, for example `a*np.exp(-x/tau) + c` or
  `amplitude*np.sin(frequency*x + phase)`.
- Constants from `scipy.constants` through `co`, for example `co.k*x`.
- Gaussian components `G1(x)`, `G2(x)`, and so on. Each creates parameters
  `G1_a`, `G1_mu`, and `G1_sigma`.
- Lorentzian components `L1(x)`, `L2(x)`, and so on. Each creates parameters
  `L1_a`, `L1_x0`, `L1_gamma`, and `L1_c`.

Changing the expression updates the initial-parameter inputs automatically.
Parameter names may contain more than one character. The reported uncertainty
after `±` is the standard error calculated from the fit covariance matrix.

## Fitting line cuts

1. Draw and extract a line from the map.
2. In the Line Cut Plotter, right-click the desired line and select `Fit Model`.
3. Enter the model expression, initial values, and `maxfev`.
4. Select `Fit Selected Line`. The fitted curve is added to the line-cut list,
   and the parameters and uncertainties remain visible in the fitting window.

## Fitting traces

1. Open a file with `Plotting > Plot Map with Trace Data`.
2. Select `Tools > Fit Traces`.
3. Select a preview trace with the X- and Y-index spinboxes.
4. Enter the same model expression, initial values, and `maxfev` used for a
   line-cut fit.
5. Select `Fit Preview` to check the model and starting values.
6. Select `Run on all Traces` to fit every trace. The preview result is used as
   the starting point, and each successful fit initializes the next trace.
7. When fitting finishes, select a fitted parameter as a map input channel.

`maxfev` is the maximum number of model evaluations allowed for each fit. Raise
it when a valid model converges slowly; lowering it can shorten failed fits.
